    {"name": "has_processed_date", "analytical_usefulness": 0.35, "source_groundedness": 0.95, "rationale": "Presence of a processed_date could weakly relate to whether an expense was handled/declined, giving a coarse group axis, but it is a generic presence flag trivially derivable from the existing processed_date column and only loosely tied to the decline query. Values correctly mirror the source (1 when a date exists, 0 for nan).", "hallucination_observed": false},
    {"name": "is_customer_support_travel", "analytical_usefulness": 0.55, "source_groundedness": 0.6, "rationale": "A department/type-scoped travel flag is a plausible new axis to compare decline rates across travel origins, offering some analytical leverage for the query. However, none of the sampled rows are travel expenses (they are hardware/cloud asset entries) so groundedness cannot be strongly confirmed; the flag appears derivable from category/department but evidence is thin and all sampled values are 0.", "hallucination_observed": false},
    {"name": "is_sales_travel", "analytical_usefulness": 0.55, "source_groundedness": 0.6, "rationale": "Same rationale as the customer-support travel flag: a reasonable grouping axis for comparing decline likelihood by travel type, but the samples are all non-travel asset rows so the flag's derivation is only weakly evidenced, and it partially overlaps conceptually with the sibling travel flag.", "hallucination_observed": false}
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
  "source_groundedness": 0.7,
  "rationale": "The two travel-type flags provide a modest new axis for comparing decline patterns across travel categories, which is relevant to the query, though they are near-constant (mostly 0) and partly redundant with each other and with existing category/department fields. has_processed_date is a generic presence flag with weak tie to the decline question. Groundedness is decent where checkable, but the sampled rows are all non-travel asset entries, so the travel flags' derivation is only partially evidenced; no invented facts observed."
}

